+# Specialty Directory Assembled as an In-Memory Read Model
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+REQ-SPEC-001 adds a page that inverts the veterinarian directory: each specialty with the veterinarians holding it. The stored association runs one way. `Vet` owns a unidirectional `@ManyToMany` to `Specialty`, and `Specialty` knows nothing of `Vet`.
+
+The page must also list a specialty no veterinarian holds, and must omit a veterinarian holding none. `spring.jpa.open-in-view` is off, so every value the view reads is loaded before rendering. The veterinarian read is already cached and already fetches specialties eagerly.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Make the association bidirectional** — add an inverse `@ManyToMany` to `Specialty`. Rejected: it widens a persisted entity for one read-only page, and an eager inverse side changes the fetch plan of every existing veterinarian lookup.
+2. **A JPQL projection query joining the two tables** — rejected: the left join needed for unheld specialties repeats the specialty row per holder, and its ordering follows each vendor's collation. That is the divergence class already recorded under Known Defects.
+3. **Invert in memory from the two reads the application already makes** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+The directory is a read model assembled in memory. A static factory takes the specialty collection and the veterinarian collection and returns the grouped, ordered result. Assembly is a pure function, unit-testable with no Spring context and no database.
+
+The read model carries copied name values rather than entity references, so it is immutable and equal by value.
+
+Order is fixed in Java rather than in SQL: specialties by name, then veterinarians by last name and first name. One comparator produces one order on all three database vendors. `Vet.getSpecialties()` already sorts by name in Java.
+
+The controller binds the route, calls the factory, and selects the view. It carries no rule, so the deviation recorded against the *Web controller* pattern does not grow.
+
+The specialty repository is not cached. Veterinarian-cache eviction is an open question, and a second cache would widen it.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Both collections are read in full on every request. Their size is bounded by clinic staff, and the veterinarian read is served from cache.
+- A specialty no veterinarian holds renders with an empty holder list. Every seeded specialty is held, so only a test with controlled repositories observes that case.
+- A veterinarian holding no specialty is absent, because grouping is driven by the specialties each one holds.
+- `Specialty` is unchanged, so the veterinarian list's fetch plan is untouched.
+- This is the first assembly logic in the codebase placed outside a controller. It sets the precedent for later read models.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-SPEC-001
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts)
+- [Feature-Package Organization Without a Service Layer](2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md)
+- [No Entry Point to the Specialty Directory](2026-08-08-non-goal-specialty-directory-entry-point.md)
